<header>
	<h1>Install language packs to translate user-facing text.</h1>
	<p>
		These language packs provide translations for text on the user portal and in automatic emails sent to users.
		Some packs also include agent or admin interface translations.
	</p>
</header>
<article>
	<section>
		<p>
			You can set the language to use for a ticket or a user in the agent interface.
		</p>
		<p>
			Users can select which of the installed languages to see on the portal.
		</p>
		<p>
			You can change the language of tickets or users in bulk under <strong>Settings &amp; Tools</strong>.
		</p>
		<p>
			If you need to support as many languages as possible, <strong>Settings &amp; Tools</strong>
			has an option to install and enable new language packs automatically, as they become available.
		</p>
		<p>You will need to enter translations for custom resources like department names, custom field names, etc. using the <strong>Edit Phrases</strong> button.</p>
		<p>You can also use <strong>Edit Phrases</strong> to enter custom phrases. This enables you to customize any text users or agents will see in the software interface and in emails.</p>
		<p>The template system controls the text of the portal and automatic emails by including these phrases within its templates. So to customize text, you must first create a custom phrase, then edit the relevant template to use your custom phrase.</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2><span>FAQ</span></h2>
		<dl>
			<dt><em>Q:</em>Can I translate DeskPRO into my own language?</dt>
			<dd><em>A:</em>
				Yes, we use a <a href="http://languages.deskpro.com/" target="_blank">crowdsourced translation system</a> to allow our customers to contribute new or improved translations. Please email <a href="mailto:support@deskpro.com?Subject=Crowdsourced%20translation%20access">support@deskpro.com</a> to request access.
			</dd>
		</dl>
	</section>
